# The vault's schema, as data. Phase 8.
#
# Phase 4 kept this in code: `TYPES = {...}` and `if t not in TYPES: reject()`.
# Here it is a graph the reasoner can read and a query can ask about.
#
# Every property below carries `rdfs:comment` naming the query it answers.
# A property that answers no query is deleted, not documented.

@prefix v:       <https://tomato.vault/schema/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:     <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix foaf:    <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/> .
@prefix prov:    <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .

#################################################################
# Version, and the two policies that keep this file loadable
# without a network. Phase 14 Step 4.
#
# ONE FILE. Phase 14 sketched ontology/{artifact,kernel,profiles,
# provenance,alignments}.ttl - six files for 46 triples. Split when
# a real seam appears, not before. The trigger is written down:
# a second PROFILE (a band with vocabulary the rest cannot read), or
# 200 triples, whichever comes first.
#
# NO owl:imports, EVER. dcterms, skos, foaf and prov appear here as
# prefixes only; their ontology files are never pulled in. So the
# reasoner sees THIS file and nothing else, every run is reproducible
# offline, and a reused term carries exactly the local constraints
# written below - which is what made dcterms:hasPart safe to adopt
# where dcterms:isPartOf was not.
#################################################################

<https://tomato.vault/schema/> a owl:Ontology ;
    owl:versionIRI <https://tomato.vault/schema/0.1> ;
    owl:versionInfo "0.1" ;
    rdfs:comment "The vault's schema. History lives in docs/phases/ - phase08 built it, phase13 renamed the document classes, phase14 stripped every rdfs:domain." .

#################################################################
# Classes — the type hierarchy is by ROLE, not by subject.
#
# The 13 type values do not form a semantic tree; grouping
# concept/procedure/reference answers no query. What earns a
# superclass is the role a note plays: did I write it, did it
# come from elsewhere, or is it scaffolding.
#
# Every leaf carries the `Document` suffix. Phase 13 put it there.
# Before it, `v:Principle` named the FILE whose `type:` is principle —
# but that name reads as the principle itself, which is what the file
# is about, not what it is. Two resources, one name. The suffix is the
# whole fix: a name ending in Document is a note on disk, and the bare
# name is left free for the knowledge entity Phase 13 introduces.
#
# There is deliberately NO compatibility axiom. An
# `owl:equivalentClass v:Principle` would keep the old name resolving,
# and under the OWL RL closure of Phase 9 it would type every document
# as `v:Principle` again — reinstating the exact ambiguity this rename
# removes. Nothing reads the generated .vault.ttl, so nothing needs it.
#################################################################

v:Document   a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "Any note that is a node in the graph." .

v:Content    a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf v:Document ;
    rdfs:comment "A note I authored. Query: my own thinking, source-notes and scaffolding excluded." .
v:Imported   a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf v:Document ;
    rdfs:comment "A note that came from elsewhere. Query: what did I take in versus write." .
v:Structural a rdfs:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf v:Document ;
    rdfs:comment "Scaffolding, not content. Query: exclude these when counting real notes or finding orphans." .

# Content — the authored types.
v:ConceptDocument    rdfs:subClassOf v:Content .
v:ProcedureDocument  rdfs:subClassOf v:Content .
v:ReferenceDocument  rdfs:subClassOf v:Content .
v:PrincipleDocument  rdfs:subClassOf v:Content .
v:DecisionDocument   rdfs:subClassOf v:Content .
v:CaseDocument       rdfs:subClassOf v:Content .
v:LogDocument        rdfs:subClassOf v:Content .
v:ReflectionDocument rdfs:subClassOf v:Content .
v:ProjectDocument    rdfs:subClassOf v:Content .
v:TradeoffDocument   rdfs:subClassOf v:Content .

# A note ABOUT someone else's work that is nonetheless MINE: the film, game
# and show notes whose body is a review I wrote. They sat under source-note
# and therefore under v:Imported, so the query v:Content answers - my own
# thinking - was missing 141 documents. Measured 2026-08-26: 602 Frames 116
# with a 감상평 section, 603 Game 25 game notes with a 리뷰 section.
# 601 Books and 604 YouTube stay v:Imported: they are transcription, and
# 604 has a 감상 section in exactly ZERO of its 90 notes.
v:ReviewDocument     rdfs:subClassOf v:Content .

# Imported — one type.
v:SourceNoteDocument rdfs:subClassOf v:Imported .

# Structural — one type. `template` was dropped 2026-08-26: a blank form
# now carries the type of the document it produces, so nothing is left here
# but the hub.
v:HubDocument        rdfs:subClassOf v:Structural .

v:Folder a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "A directory, a resource in its own right since Phase 6." .

#################################################################
# Knowledge entities — Phase 14 Step 1.
#
# NOT the document. `_Insights.md` is one v:ReflectionDocument that
# holds 24 separate insights; `By Subquestion/` holds 27 documents
# whose TITLE is a question. One document class, three different
# kinds of thing - which is why these exist at all.
#
# Only two. The phase sketched thirteen candidates and eleven had no
# competency question behind them (Claim has no counterpart in the
# vault at all: technical assertions are written as `패턴 N:`).
#
# There is deliberately no v:KnowledgeEntity superclass. No question
# asks for "every knowledge entity", and a class that answers no
# query is not written down.
#
# MEASURED GAP, kept on purpose: `패턴` is the vault's most common
# entity - 135 items across 32 documents - and no competency question
# requests it. The questions cluster on 400/200/500 and rarely make
# 300 Runtime the subject. Not a deletion, a recorded hole: add the
# class when a question needs it.
#################################################################

v:Section a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "A heading and what it opens. Minted only for the 49 documents whose numbered item headings are addressed individually. NOT a v:Document - Phase 13 split them and the reasoner must not rejoin them." .

v:Insight a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "A belief with a subject and a time, written as `인사이트 N:`. Query: how a belief changed and what changed it (C08); which values recur (C07). A parser writes this from the heading prefix - it is never inferred." .

v:Question a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "A question I am holding open. Query: which band answers this subquestion, and which have no answer (C27); what to reread when revising a guideline (C11)." .

#################################################################
# Properties we own. Each is a subproperty of a standard, so a
# tool that knows the standard understands ours.
#################################################################

# NO domain, NO range. Both said "whatever builds_on touches is something I
# wrote" - and that is false: a note of mine can build on a source-note I only
# transcribed. Measured 2026-08-26: the two declarations typed 27 documents
# v:Content that are v:Imported or v:Structural, so the query v:Content exists
# to answer - my own thinking, source-notes excluded - was wrong by 27.
v:builds_on a owl:ObjectProperty, owl:TransitiveProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf dcterms:references ;
    rdfs:comment "Curated prerequisites. Query: what must I read before this note (q path). Declared transitive; the closure is NOT materialised until Phase 9." .

# NO domain, NO range. A link may point at a SECTION, and a section is not a
# document - typing it v:Document would undo the split Phase 13 just made.
# The declarations bought nothing anyway: measured 2026-08-26, zero resources
# reached v:Document by inference. Every document is typed by its own `type:`.
v:links_to a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf dcterms:references ;
    rdfs:comment "A body wikilink that resolved. Query: neighbours of a note (q near, the forward half)." .

v:linked_by a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    owl:inverseOf v:links_to ;
    rdfs:comment "The backlink. Query: what points at this note (q near, the reverse half). Not materialised until Phase 9; Phase 7 read it with ^." .

# NO domain, NO range. Folders are already typed by isPartOf's range and
# documents by their own `type:`; both declarations typed nothing (measured).
v:hub a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf foaf:primaryTopic ;
    rdfs:comment "The note that represents a folder. Query: given a folder, its landing note." .

#################################################################
# Semantic relations — Phase 14, from the 30 competency questions.
#
# snake_case, so a frontmatter key and its RDF name are ONE string
# and no lookup table sits between them. `v:builds_on` already broke
# the camelCase convention; this follows it rather than the standard.
#
# Eight, with no domain and no range. Phase 14 Step 3 measured what
# such declarations bought on the existing four properties: 27 wrong
# types and nothing right. These carry none.
#
# NOT transitive: derived_from. PROV does not hold derivation
# transitive in general, and Phase 9 measured OWL RL materialisation
# at 2.80x the triples for zero qualitatively new useful facts.
# NOT symmetric: informed_by. C09's "mutually reinforcing" is a case
# where both directions hold, not a claim that the relation is
# symmetric. If both hold, both get written.
# NO declared inverses: `^v:applies` in SPARQL already answers the
# reverse, as Phase 7 showed.
#################################################################

v:derived_from a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf prov:wasDerivedFrom ;
    rdfs:comment "A came out of B. Query: the tradeoff and source behind a decision (C02); the event behind a principle (C04); which log entry a principle was promoted from (C28). Absorbs five candidates - the target's own type already says what KIND of source it was." .

v:contradicts a owl:ObjectProperty, owl:SymmetricProperty ;
    rdfs:comment "Both cannot hold. Query: decisions whose source was later refuted (C03); the 300-209-400 triangle where judgements disagree (C06). Symmetric, so it is written once." .

v:diverges_from a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:comment "Stands on B but changed part of it. Query: where my principle departs from the author's claim (C13). NOT contradicts - having lived with it and revised it is not a denial." .

v:applies a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:comment "This judgement applied that principle. Query: decisions that applied a principle (C21); which principles a case followed (C29)." .

# v:violates was here until 2026-08-31. Two samples measured it at zero uses,
# and the excavation that followed explained why: the breaking is real - eight
# Learnings documents record an accounting rule, an FSD layer rule and an
# architecture rule being broken - but the SUBJECT is not the document. The
# code broke the rule; the document is the correction. Attaching v:violates to
# the document names the wrong thing. And a breach found while writing gets
# reconciled INTO the writing, so no edge survives to be stored. What stays is
# detection at authoring time, which is Phase 17's business, not a fact here.
# Cost, accepted knowingly: C29 splits - `applied` is now separable from
# `mentioned`, `applied` from `broke` is not.

# Renamed from v:informs on 2026-08-31. The old name was ACTIVE - "A informs B"
# reads as A giving to B - while the Korean test sentence said the opposite,
# "B flowed into A". The mismatch was invisible in Korean and it made the
# subPropertyOf below plainly wrong: prov:wasInfluencedBy is PASSIVE, its
# subject being the thing that was affected. A name that is wrong in English
# breaks the tie to the standard, not just the reading. There was no data yet,
# so the rename cost nothing.
v:informed_by a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf prov:wasInfluencedBy ;
    rdfs:comment "It fed into this. Not the ground, but it flowed in. Query: personal criteria surfacing in technical decisions (C09); a reaction becoming reflection or creation (C15)." .

v:expresses a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:comment "This judgement enacts that value. Query: values that recur across 500 and 100/600/700 (C07)." .

v:answered_by a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain v:Question ;
    rdfs:comment "This question finds its answer there. Query: which band answers a subquestion and which have none (C27); what to reread when revising a guideline (C11). The domain is the ONE that earns its place: writing answered_by is what makes something a question." .

#################################################################
# States. Values on a document or a fact, not relations.
#################################################################

v:status a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:comment "Query: what did I set down and stop pursuing (C17)." .

v:archived a owl:Thing ;
    rdfs:comment "Interest faded. NOT deprecated (superseded) and NOT invalid (refuted) - this vault follows PARA and 900 is a place you come back from. The other two are absent because no question asks for them: C12 was rejected for exactly this reason." .

v:source_unknown a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:comment "I looked and found nothing. Query: past documents whose source cannot be recovered (C02, C13). In RDF a missing triple means UNKNOWN; this one says SEARCHED AND ABSENT, which is a different fact and the open world assumption's whole point." .

v:as_of a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf dcterms:date ;
    rdfs:comment "When the thing described happened - dcterms:created is when I wrote it down. Query: a belief's state at each point in time (C08)." .

v:needs_review a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:comment "Query: what should be revisited now (C30); which beliefs to ask 'do you still hold this' about (C08). Must be retractable, so it is a value and not a relation." .

#################################################################
# Standard properties we reuse directly.
#
# A local domain/range scopes a reused term to this vault without
# changing its meaning elsewhere - but only TWO survive the test
# "is that sentence true?" (Phase 14 Step 3, measured 2026-08-26):
#
#   isPartOf's object is always a folder    true   -> types 773
#   subject's object is always a tag        true   -> types 245
#   builds_on's object is always mine       FALSE  -> mistyped 27
#   links_to's object is always a document  false once sections land
#
# Everything else typed nothing at all, because a document is already
# typed by its own `type:`. A declaration that infers nothing is not
# free: it is a wrong inference waiting for the data to arrive.
#################################################################

# NO rdfs:domain here on purpose. isPartOf is shared: a document is part of
# a folder, and a folder is part of its parent folder. A domain of v:Document
# would fire on the folder subjects too and mistype 764 nested folders as
# Documents - inference, not validation (measured, Phase 9). The range stays:
# every object of isPartOf is a folder, which is what types folders v:Folder.
dcterms:isPartOf rdfs:range v:Folder ;
    rdfs:comment "Folder membership, derived from the path. Query: every note under a folder." .

# The direction is reversed on purpose. `<section> dcterms:isPartOf <doc>`
# would hit the range above and type the DOCUMENT a v:Folder. Turning it
# around costs nothing and the range then types sections for free, exactly
# as isPartOf's range types folders. No domain: a folder may one day have
# parts too, and that is the mistake Phase 9 made one line up.
dcterms:hasPart rdfs:range v:Section ;
    rdfs:comment "A heading inside a document. Query: which items a document holds, and which item a semantic fact points at." .

# The range STAYS - every object of subject is a tag, which is what types 245
# tags skos:Concept. The domain went: a document is already typed by `type:`.
dcterms:subject  rdfs:range skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:comment "A tag the note carries. The tag is a skos:Concept, so its hierarchy is walkable. Query: notes on a topic, nesting included (q tag)." .

dcterms:replaces
    rdfs:comment "This note supersedes an older one. Often points at a deleted note, which is the normal case, not a fault." .

dcterms:created
    rdfs:comment "When the note was made. Central to the dated types, incidental to the rest." .

dcterms:abstract
    rdfs:comment "The note's own summary line. Required of the content types by the schema of record." .
